.TH DUP 2 "May 13, 1986"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
dup, dup2 \- duplicate a descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <unistd.h>

int
dup(\fIint fildes\fP)

int
dup2(\fIint fildes\fP, \fIint fildes2\fP)
.fi
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dup
duplicates an existing object descriptor and returns its value to the
calling process (\fIfildes2\fP = \fBdup\fP(\fIfildes\fP)).
The argument \fIfildes\fP is a small non-negative integer index in
the per-process descriptor table.  The value must be less
than the size of the table, which is returned by
.BR getdtablesize (2).
The new descriptor returned by the call
is the lowest numbered descriptor that is
not currently in use by the process.
.PP
The object referenced by the descriptor does not distinguish
between references using \fIfildes2\fP and \fIfildes\fP in any way.
Thus if \fIfildes2\fP and \fIfildes\fP are duplicate references to an open
file,
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2)
and
.BR lseek (2)
calls all move a single pointer into the file,
and append mode, non-blocking I/O and asynchronous I/O options
are shared between the references.
If a separate pointer into the file is desired, a different
object reference to the file must be obtained by issuing an
additional
.BR open (2)
call.
The close-on-exec flag on the new file descriptor is unset.
.PP
In \fBdup2\fP() the value of the new descriptor
.IR fildes2
is specified.  If 
.IR fildes
and
.IR fildes2
are equal then \fBdup2\fP() just returns
.IR fildes2 ;
no other changes are made to the existing descriptor.
Otherwise if descriptor 
.IR fildes2
is already in use, it is first deallocated as if a
.BR close (2)
call had been done first.
.SH "RETURN VALUE
The value \-1 is returned if an error occurs in either call.
The external variable
.I errno
indicates the cause of the error.
.SH "ERRORS
.B dup
and
.B dup2
will fail if:
.TP 15
[EBADF]
\fIfildes\fP is not an active valid file descriptor
[EINTR]
Execution is interrupted by a signal.
.TP 15
[EMFILE]
Too many descriptors are active.
.PP
\fBdup2\fP will fail if:
.TP 15
[EBADF]
\fIfildes2\fP is negative or greater than the maximum allowable 
number (see \fBgetdtablesize\fP(2)).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
accept(2),
open(2),
close(2),
fcntl(2),
getdtablesize(2),
pipe(2),
socket(2),
socketpair(2)
